iterT -- SEQ 484 (Tony, 2026-10-30): iterate on the new road, PRE-REGISTERED ON TRUNK. Rows prefixed IR- (itRT owns IT-).

iterate only sets up a cursor over a source; WHILe and ++ / -- do the looping and stepping. This file is not run alone:
pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm -- everything above this file's `stop();` (fsrc gets fa fb fc
by its last line), then the row on the OLD road or on the NEW road (STARt by name, then testOP, except IR-0, then the optional
4th field, old-road statements after the call), then `IR NEXT` and a sentinel. The cursor ic is DECLARED: measured 2026-10-30,
the declared cursor walks all three on the old road (bear-trap 38's one-member walk did not show at the top level).

MEASURED ON THE OLD ROAD, taken as intended: ++ walks 1 2 3, -- walks 3 2 1; an empty source prints nothing (two refusals: the
source has no list, ++ has no value to bump); break at 2 reads 1 2; after the loop `print *ic` prints nothing.
TODAY on the new road: ITERATe has no action (and is still defer), STARt skips it, and ++ on a never-assigned ic COUNTS (1, 2,
...) where the old road refuses -- so `while ++ic` never ends: every IR-1..5 runs to the alarm (exit 142). The new-road runs
are bounded at 5 s for that reason.

IR-P (SEQ 486 R1): ++ on a never-assigned field refuses on the new road as on the old, and the next statement runs. It
counted before SEQ 486: building any new-road expression naming the field wrote a 0 count into it (opDot's 0 for a data-less
product landed on the real node a .nexT walk returned); opDot now gives that 0 only to a product it mints.

